1. What are File Permissions?

File permissions determine who can access a file or directory and what actions they can perform.

Linux is a multi-user operating system, so permissions are used to protect files from unauthorized access.

For example:

Only the owner can modify an important configuration file.
Other users can only read it.
Some users may not have any access at all.

2. Why are File Permissions Important?

File permissions help to:

Protect sensitive data
Prevent accidental deletion or modification
Control user access
Improve system security

3. Real-Life Example

Imagine your house.

Owner → You (can enter, modify, and lock the house)
Family Members → Can enter but cannot make major changes.
Visitors → May only be allowed to enter or may not be allowed at all.

4. Permission Categories (Who?)

Every file and directory has three permission categories.

| Category | Symbol | Meaning                     |
| -------- | ------ | --------------------------- |
| Owner    | `u`    | User who owns the file      |
| Group    | `g`    | Members of the file's group |
| Others   | `o`    | Everyone else on the system |

Example

Owner    Group    Others
   ↓         ↓         ↓
-rwxr-xr--

5. Permission Types (What?)

There are three basic permissions.

| Permission | Symbol | Value | Meaning                           |
| ---------- | ------ | ----: | --------------------------------- |
| Read       | `r`    |     4 | View the contents of a file       |
| Write      | `w`    |     2 | Modify a file                     |
| Execute    | `x`    |     1 | Run a file as a program or script |

6. Understanding Permission Output

Command
ls -l

Output
-rwxr-xr-- 1 bilal developers 1200 Aug 1

Breakdown:
-rwxr-xr--

│
├── First character
│      -
│
├── Owner
│      rwx
│
├── Group
│      r-x
│
└── Others
       r--

7. First Character Meaning

| Symbol | Meaning          |
| ------ | ---------------- |
| `-`    | Regular file     |
| `d`    | Directory        |
| `l`    | Symbolic link    |
| `c`    | Character device |
| `b`    | Block device     |

8. Changing Permissions Using chmod

chmod means Change Mode.

Syntax
chmod permissions file_name

Numeric Method

Example
chmod 755 backup.sh

9. Symbolic Method

Add Execute Permission
chmod +x backup.sh

Remove Write Permission
chmod -w backup.sh

Give Only Owner Execute Permission
chmod u+x backup.sh

Remove Execute Permission from Others
chmod o-x backup.sh

Give Group Write Permission
chmod g+w project.txt

10. Understanding u, g, o, and a

Symbol	Meaning
u	User (Owner)
g	Group
o	Others
a	All (User + Group + Others)

11. Directory Permissions

Directory permissions behave differently.

| Permission | Meaning                                               |
| ---------- | ----------------------------------------------------- |
| Read       | List the directory contents (`ls`)                    |
| Write      | Create, delete, or rename files inside the directory  |
| Execute    | Enter the directory (`cd`) and access files within it |

12. Changing Ownership
chown

Change the owner.

Example

sudo chown bilal notes.txt
Owner becomes bilal.

Change Owner and Group
sudo chown bilal:developers notes.txt

13. Changing Group

Use chgrp.

Example

sudo chgrp developers notes.txt
